From 88c2ab42c49a47f6ff490f960fd09bf62463c7af Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Thomas Bechtold <thomas.bechtold@chainguard.dev>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 15:20:53 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Add an OpenSSL backend for GChecksum and GHmac

With -Dopenssl=true, GChecksum and GHmac are implemented with OpenSSL
libcrypto (EVP_MD and the "HMAC" EVP_MAC) instead of GLib's built-in
MD5/SHA code. Algorithms are fetched from the default library context,
so the process-wide OpenSSL configuration decides which provider
computes them. With a FIPS configuration all hashing and HMAC done
through GLib (and its users, e.g. libsoup) runs inside the FIPS
provider.

libcrypto is dlopen()ed by soname rather than linked, so that
libglib-2.0.a can still be linked without a static libcrypto, and
processes that never compute a checksum do not load it. Only the
OpenSSL headers are needed at build time; the function pointer types
come from them, so prototype changes fail the build.

Behaviour differences from the built-in backend:

- g_checksum_new() and g_hmac_new() return NULL (with a warning) when
  the configured providers do not offer the algorithm, e.g. MD5 in a
  strict FIPS configuration, or reject the HMAC key, e.g. keys shorter
  than 112 bits under the FIPS key check.
- A libcrypto that cannot be loaded, or a failure in an operation that
  cannot report errors (update, finalise, copy), aborts the process
  with g_error() rather than silently using unvalidated code or
  returning a wrong digest.
- OpenSSL errors are cleared from the thread's error queue after they
  are reported, so other OpenSSL users in the process (glib-networking)
  do not see stale errors.

The built-in GHmac core in ghmac.c is compiled out with -Dopenssl; the
g_compute_hmac_*() helpers there only use the public API and are shared
by both backends.
